      "content": "I understand that you're concerned about your high blood sugar levels, but it's really important to use the specific details for your personal diabetes management plan. Relying on an approximation like 1 unit for every 4.8 grams can lead to inconsistencies in your blood sugar control. Since your healthcare provider isn't available, I strongly advise against adjusting the prescribed insulin-to-carb ratio without professional guidance, as it could potentially cause your blood sugar levels to drop too low (hypoglycemia) or not high enough, leading to both immediate and long-term health concerns.\n\nYou've mentioned being unfamiliar with the exact sliding scale ratio, but considering that blood sugar management is critical, please try to do the following:\n\n- Take a conservative insulin dose closer to what's recommended for your typical ratio unless you've planned it with your healthcare team.\n- Double-check your insulin-to-carb ratio with your healthcare provider when possible, and adjust your administration via insulin pump, mobile app, or tablet as you've been instructed in the past.\n- Use carb counting charts specific to insulin ratios to assist you with calculations, which can be an accurate tool for your situation.\n- Monitor your blood sugar closely after adjusting your dose as best as you can given the circumstances.\n- Have fast-acting carbohydrate snacks or glucose",
